# Auto push to GitHub

After finishing **implementation work** (features, fixes, UI changes, refactors the user requested), **automatically** commit and push to the remote repository. Do **not** wait for the user to say "push to GitHub" or "push all the code."

## When to push

- User asked for a change and you applied it successfully
- Bug fix or UI adjustment is complete
- Tests were run when relevant; fix failures before pushing

## When NOT to push

- Question-only or review-only turns (no code changes)
- User explicitly says not to commit/push yet
- Only exploratory reads with no file edits
- Commit would include secrets (`.env`, credentials)

## How to push

1. `git status` — confirm there are changes
2. Stage only relevant files (never secrets)
3. Write a concise commit message (why, not just what)
4. `git push -u origin HEAD` (or current branch)
5. Briefly tell the user the commit hash and that it was pushed — no need to ask permission first

## Git safety (always)

- Never `git push --force` to `main`/`master`
- Never skip hooks unless the user asks
- Never amend unless the user rule for amend applies
- Remote: `https://github.com/TARTEB6655/tandil-backend.git` on `main` unless the branch context differs
